Below is a step‐by‐step reasoning and the resulting goal model decomposition for the multi‐robot resource collection and delivery mission described.

──────────────────────────────
Step 1. Identify the Mission Phases and Key Actions

• The mission has two main phases:  
 – Collection Phase: The robot goes to one or more storages, navigates there, sends a precise resource request, and waits for the resources to be retrieved.  
 – Delivery Phase: Once resources are collected, the robot makes one or more runs to deliver them to a specified destination.  

• There are conditional “fallback” behaviors:  
 – In the Collection Phase, if the robot’s battery falls below 10%, it must go back to recharge and reassign the mission to another robot.  
 – In the Delivery Phase, if the battery is below 30%, the robot must return the resource to a checkpoint and reassign the remaining delivery. If returning to the checkpoint fails, an alert must be triggered and a report sent to the sector manager.

• When multiple items are required from different storages, the mission may be executed in parallel by multiple robots.

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. Define the Overall Goal and Its Children

We define a top‐level goal (G1) that represents the overall mission. Its two major children are the Collection Phase (G1.1) and the Delivery Phase (G1.2). These phases must be executed sequentially (AND decomposition) because delivery cannot start before collection.

Under Collection (G1.1), we define a sub‐goal (G1.1.1) that covers the “Approach and Request Resource” process. This sub‐goal is performed in sequence (navigate to storage, request resource, wait for retrieval) but includes a fallback action (Recharge and Reassign) if the battery is low.

Under Delivery (G1.2), we define a sub‐goal (G1.2.1) that represents a “Deliver Resource Run.” This run includes navigating to the destination and delivering the resource. It also has a fallback branch that first attempts to “Return Resource to Checkpoint” and, if that fails, triggers an “Alert and Report.”

